          "outcome": "Explain and use Ohm’s law for one simple series circuit. Stop once I can solve and explain one changed circuit independently.",
          "explanation": "The accepted outcome is small and self-contained: explain Ohm's law and apply it to one simple series circuit, then stop after one independent changed circuit. Readiness evidence is absent (no completed work), so a beginner needs the prerequisite constructs of voltage, current, resistance and the series relationship before applying the law. Rather than a long sequence, one integrated activity can teach the constructs, demonstrate the law, walk through one worked example, and then have the learner solve a changed circuit independently. That single activity supplies both the learning and the independent evidence the outcome requires. No separate practice set or second check is needed because the changed example itself is the independent check. The preferred 30-minute timebox fits this comfortably; no phases are required for such a bounded outcome.",
