            "likelyMinutes": 35,
            "highMinutes": 45
          },
          "phases": []
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The entire accepted outcome: explaining and applying Ohm's law to one simple series circuit, assessed by one independent changed circuit."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "ohm_law_series",
            "outcomeRefs": [],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Learn and apply Ohm's law to a simple series circuit",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation and independent application",
            "purpose": "Teach the needed constructs and Ohm's law, then verify independent use on a changed simple series circuit.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in order. (1) Read the brief explanation below. (2) Follow the worked example. (3) Solve the changed circuit yourself without help, then check your work against the answer key. Do not skip the independent step.\n\nExplanation:\n- Voltage (V, in volts) is the electrical push across a component.\n- Current (I, in amperes) is the flow of charge through the circuit.\n- Resistance (R, in ohms) opposes the current.\n- Ohm's law: V = I × R. Rearranged: I = V / R and R = V / I.\n- In a simple series circuit, the same current flows through every component, and the source voltage equals the sum of the voltages across the components.\n\nWorked example:\nA 12 V battery is connected to two resistors in series: R1 = 4 Ω and R2 = 2 Ω.\nStep 1: Total resistance R = R1 + R2 = 4 + 2 = 6 Ω.\nStep 2: Current I = V / R = 12 / 6 = 2 A.\nStep 3: Voltage across R1 = I × R1 = 2 × 4 = 8 V.\nStep 4: Voltage across R2 = I × R2 = 2 × 2 = 4 V.\nCheck: 8 + 4 = 12 V, matching the battery. Correct.\n\nYour changed circuit (solve independently):\nA 9 V battery is connected to two resistors in series: R1 = 2 Ω and R2 = 4 Ω.\n(a) Find the total resistance.\n(b) Find the current.\n(c) Find the voltage across each resistor.\n(d) Check that the two voltages add to the battery voltage.\n(e) In 2–3 sentences, explain why the current is the same through both resistors and how Ohm's law gave you the current.\n\nAnswer key (use only after attempting):\n(a) R = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω.\n(b) I = 9 / 6 = 1.5 A.\n(c) V across R1 = 1.5 × 2 = 3 V; V across R2 = 1.5 × 4 = 6 V.\n(d) 3 + 6 = 9 V, matching the battery.\n(e) In a series circuit the same current flows through every component, so one current value applies to both resistors; Ohm's law I = V / R uses the total voltage and total resistance to give that current.",
            "completionCriterion": "You correctly find total resistance, current, and each resistor's voltage for the changed 9 V circuit, verify the voltages sum to 9 V, and explain in your own words why the current is the same through both resistors and how Ohm's law was used.",
