providerResponses.text:
{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can distinguish current from potential difference, explain why current is conserved at a junction and voltage is shared around a series loop, and solve one new mixed circuit problem unaided with correct reasoning."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "I confuse current with potential difference and cannot explain why circuit rules hold. Prepare for independent problem solving.",
          "explanation": "The reported weakness is conceptual: current and potential difference are conflated, and the rules are memorised rather than derived. Independent problem solving therefore rests on two conceptual foundations first: a correct energy-based mental model of potential difference versus a flow-based model of current, and a derivation of junction and loop rules from charge conservation and energy conservation. Only once those hold is a method-selection and application task worthwhile. Because no assessment weights or past performance exist, this is a short conceptual rebuild plus one integrated independent problem, not a large cram. Later technique drills, timed practice and paper analysis are deferred until the first independent problem reveals which specific errors remain.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 90,
            "likelyMinutes": 135,
            "highMinutes": 200
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "concepts",
              "title": "Conceptual foundations",
              "outcome": "Correctly distinguish current from potential difference and derive the junction and loop rules from conservation principles.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 60,
                "likelyMinutes": 90,
                "highMinutes": 130
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "You can state the defining relation and units of each quantity, give a physical reason why current is conserved at a junction and why potential differences sum around a loop, and use both quantities correctly in one worked example."
            },
            {
              "localId": "apply",
              "title": "Independent problem solving",
              "outcome": "Choose a valid method and solve a changed mixed circuit problem unaided, justifying each step with the conservation rules.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 30,
                "likelyMinutes": 45,
                "highMinutes": 70
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "A correct unaided solution to a new circuit with a stated valid reason for each rule applied and no assistance."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Rebuilds the distinction between current (rate of charge flow, conserved at a junction) and potential difference (energy transferred per unit charge, shared around a loop), which is the reported confusion and the prerequisite for every circuit rule."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Derives why the rules hold: charge conservation gives the junction rule and energy conservation gives the loop rule, converting memorised statements into explainable principles."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The accepted outcome itself: one changed mixed circuit problem solved independently, selecting and justifying a valid method, which is the evidence that the conceptual rebuild transfers to problem solving."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "concept_build",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "concepts"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2"
            ],
            "title": "Rebuild current vs potential difference and derive the circuit conservation rules",
            "activityType": "Guided conceptual study with derivation",
            "purpose": "Repair the reported confusion by separating the two quantities and grounding the junction and loop rules in conservation principles.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting. (1) Write the defining relation for current (I = ΔQ/Δt, unit ampere) and for potential difference (V = ΔW/ΔQ, unit volt). In your own words, explain why current is about how much charge passes per second at a point, while potential difference is about energy transferred per unit charge between two points. (2) Draw a single loop with a battery and two resistors. Mark the current through each element and the potential difference across each. Explain why the current is the same at every point of this series loop (charge cannot pile up) and why the potential differences across the resistors add to the battery potential difference (energy per unit charge is conserved around the loop). (3) Draw a junction where one wire splits into two. Explain why the current entering equals the total current leaving (charge conservation at a junction). (4) Write the two rules in your own words and, for each, name the conservation law it comes from. (5) Check yourself: for a 12 V battery in series with a 4 ohm and a 2 ohm resistor, compute the current and the potential difference across each resistor, and state which rule you used at each step. Expected results: I = 2 A; V across 4 ohm = 8 V; V across 2 ohm = 4 V; 8 V + 4 V = 12 V. If any of these do not match, redo that step and write down the exact correction.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written both defining relations with units, explained the junction and loop rules from charge and energy conservation in your own words, and your worked series check gives I = 2 A with potential differences 8 V and 4 V summing to 12 V.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 60,
              "likelyMinutes": 90,
              "highMinutes": 130
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 90,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": []
          },
          {
            "localId": "independent_problem",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "apply"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Solve a changed mixed circuit problem independently",
            "activityType": "Independent problem solving",
            "purpose": "Provides the evidence that the conceptual rebuild transfers to unaided problem solving and reveals any remaining specific error.",
            "instructions": "Solve this problem without help, using only the concepts and rules you rebuilt in the previous activity. Circuit: a 9 V battery is connected to a 3 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair made of a 6 ohm and a 3 ohm resistor. (a) State which rules you will use and why they apply here. (b) Find the equivalent resistance of the parallel pair and then the total resistance of the circuit. (c) Find the total current from the battery and the current through each branch of the parallel pair. (d) Find the potential difference across the series resistor and across the parallel pair. (e) Check that the currents at the junction satisfy the junction rule and that the potential differences around the loop satisfy the loop rule. Expected results: parallel pair equivalent resistance = 2 ohm; total resistance = 5 ohm; total current = 1.8 A; current through 6 ohm = 0.6 A and through 3 ohm = 1.2 A (sum 1.8 A); potential difference across series resistor = 5.4 V and across parallel pair = 3.6 V (sum 9 V). After solving, mark your work against these results. For every step that differs, write the correct step and the reason for the error. Do not redo steps that were already correct.",
            "completionCriterion": "An unaided solution with the correct method stated and justified, correct values (2 ohm, 5 ohm, 1.8 A, 0.6 A, 1.2 A, 5.4 V, 3.6 V), and a check showing the junction and loop rules are satisfied; any incorrect step is corrected with a written reason.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 30,
              "likelyMinutes": 45,
              "highMinutes": 70
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 45,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "concept_build"
            ],
            "stoppingCondition": "Stop once the problem is solved and checked and any incorrect step has been corrected with a written reason; further similar problems are not required now."
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has basic algebra and has met Ohm's law before, so the work is conceptual repair rather than first exposure to the equation.",
            "No assessment weights, past papers or performance data exist, so effort is apportioned by conceptual necessity rather than by exam weighting."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "Deferred: extended technique drills, timed circuit practice and past-paper error analysis. They are only useful after the first independent problem shows which specific errors remain, so committing time to them now would be speculative."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "Whether the learner also confuses series and parallel combination rules; the independent problem will reveal this, and further work is contingent on that evidence.",
            "Whether the learner's algebra is secure enough to isolate variables; if the independent problem fails only on algebra, the conceptual rebuild still stands and algebra support would be added."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop after the independent problem is solved, checked and any incorrect step corrected; do not add further practice unless the check reveals a specific unresolved error."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "This plan commits about 135 minutes over two connected activities: a conceptual rebuild of current versus potential difference and the conservation rules, then one changed independent problem that tests transfer. Later technique drills, timed practice and paper analysis are deliberately deferred because no assessment weights exist and the first independent problem will show which specific errors, if any, need targeted work; committing to them now would be speculative rather than educational."
}

