            "lowMinutes": 45,
            "likelyMinutes": 70,
            "highMinutes": 100
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "selection",
              "title": "Method selection on unfamiliar resistive circuits",
              "outcome": "Correctly choose and apply a method, with a valid reason, on resistive circuits not seen before.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 45,
                "likelyMinutes": 70,
                "highMinutes": 100
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "On three unfamiliar circuits, name the candidate methods, choose one with a valid assumption/topology reason, and obtain a correct solution with a check, without help."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The whole scope is method selection on unfamiliar circuits; it is addressed by the integrated selection activity and, if needed, the contingent remediation."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "select_apply",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "selection"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Choose and apply a circuit method on unfamiliar resistive circuits",
            "activityType": "Guided method-selection practice with independent changed examples",
            "purpose": "Build and test the ability to pick a valid method on unfamiliar circuits, the exact reported weakness.",
            "instructions": "Use only these methods, which are the taught repertoire for this task: Ohm's law; series and parallel reduction; voltage divider; current divider; Kirchhoff's current law (KCL); Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL). Do not use nodal, mesh, superposition, Thevenin or Norton analysis unless you already know them, and treat them as optional extras, not required.\n\nStep 1 - Selection rules (write these in your own words):\n- Two-terminal network with one source and resistors in simple series/parallel: reduce to a single equivalent resistance, then use Ohm's law.\n- Need a voltage across one resistor in a series string: voltage divider.\n- Need the current through one resistor in a parallel branch: current divider.\n- Circuit has multiple sources or a resistor shared by two loops: use KCL and/or KVL with the fewest unknowns.\n- A method is valid only if its assumptions hold: check that the topology and source arrangement actually match the method before using it.\n\nStep 2 - Worked changed example (I supply the data):\nCircuit A: 12 V source; R1 = 4 ohm in series with a parallel pair R2 = 12 ohm and R3 = 6 ohm. Find the current through R3.\n- Candidate methods: series/parallel reduction + Ohm's law, or current divider.\n- Valid choice: reduce R2||R3 = (12*6)/(12+6) = 4 ohm; total = 4 + 4 = 8 ohm; total current = 12/8 = 1.5 A; current through R3 by current divider = 1.5 * 12/(12+6) = 1.0 A. Check with voltage divider: voltage across the parallel pair = 12 * 4/8 = 6 V; current through R3 = 6/6 = 1.0 A. Both agree.\n- Selection reason: the parallel pair's topology matches the current divider or reduction method, so either is valid; nodal analysis would also work but is not needed.\n\nStep 3 - Independent set on unfamiliar circuits (no help). For each, name candidate methods, choose one with a one-sentence valid reason, solve, and check by a second method or by substituting back:\n1. 9 V source; R1 = 3 ohm in series with R2 = 6 ohm. Find the voltage across R2.\n2. 10 V source; R1 = 5 ohm in series with a parallel pair R2 = 10 ohm and R3 = 10 ohm. Find the current through R2.\n3. Two-loop circuit: 6 V source in loop 1 with R1 = 2 ohm and shared R2 = 4 ohm; loop 2 has R3 = 3 ohm and no source. Find the current through R2.\n\nStep 4 - After solving, compare your chosen method and reason with the selection rules. If any choice was invalid or any solution wrong, correct it and state the corrected method and reason. If all three are correct with valid reasons, you are done; do not create extra sets.",
            "completionCriterion": "All three independent circuits are solved with a named valid method, a one-sentence valid selection reason, a correct numeric answer, and a check; any actual error is corrected with the corrected method and reason stated. (Reference answers: 1) 6 V; 2) 0.5 A; 3) 0.5 A through R2.)",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 45,
              "likelyMinutes": 70,
              "highMinutes": 100
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 90,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 30,
                "completionCriterion": "Selection rules written in own words and Circuit A worked and checked."
              },
              {
                "afterMinutes": 65,
                "completionCriterion": "Independent circuits 1 and 2 solved with valid method choices, reasons and checks."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "localId": "remediate_selection",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "selection"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted remediation on the specific wrong method choices",
            "activityType": "Failure-triggered targeted method-selection correction",
            "purpose": "Only if the independent set shows a wrong method choice, fix that specific selection error with one contrasting example.",
            "instructions": "This runs only if, in the independent set of 'Choose and apply a circuit method on unfamiliar resistive circuits', you chose an invalid method or gave an invalid selection reason on at least one circuit. Take the exact circuit(s) where the choice was wrong and: (a) name the method you chose and why its assumptions/topology did not apply; (b) name the valid method and the topology/assumption that makes it valid; (c) re-solve that circuit correctly and check it. Then solve one new contrasting circuit of the same type with a valid choice and reason. Stop when that new circuit is correct with a valid reason; do not re-solve circuits that were already correct.",
            "completionCriterion": "The originally wrong circuit(s) are re-solved with a valid method and reason, and one new contrasting circuit of the same type is solved correctly with a valid reason, without help.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 30,
              "highMinutes": 45
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 45,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "select_apply",
                "condition": "On at least one circuit in the independent set of select_apply, the chosen method or its selection reason is invalid, or the solution is wrong."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "select_apply"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "No taught method repertoire, resources, past papers or assessment constraints were supplied, so the repertoire is limited to Ohm's law, series/parallel reduction, voltage and current dividers, and KCL/KVL, and the example data is supplied in the instructions."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "No timed paper, error log or broad topic review is booked because no exam date or past paper is supplied; the work is bounded to method selection on unfamiliar resistive circuits.",
            "Advanced methods (nodal, mesh, superposition, Thevenin, Norton) are optional extras, not required, so the task stays within the taught repertoire."
