            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Use a loop and a dictionary to total a tiny expenses list",
            "activityType": "Guided beginner coding practice",
            "purpose": "Teach the shared loops/dictionary prerequisite on a tiny example so it can later support both the CSV and JSON reports.",
            "instructions": "Create a file named expenses_total.py. Use this tiny list of expenses: [('coffee', 3.50), ('tea', 2.25), ('coffee', 4.00)]. Write a loop that adds each amount to a running total. Also create a dictionary that maps each item name to its total amount, for example {'coffee': 7.50, 'tea': 2.25}. Print the overall total and the dictionary. Run the script. Then change one amount or add one item and run it again. In your own words, explain what the loop variable does and how the dictionary lookup gives you an item's total. If you get stuck, ask for a concrete hint about loops or dictionaries rather than skipping the script.",
