            "instructions": "Goal: one small script that turns an expense CSV into a per-category totals report and explains malformed rows.\n\nUse your own programming environment; any beginner-friendly language is fine. You will create the sample data yourself, because the course note does not supply example data.\n\n1. Create a file expenses.csv with a header row and these exact rows (the second row deliberately contains a quoted field with a comma; that is valid CSV, so it must be read correctly, not rejected):\ncategory,amount,note\nFood,12.50,lunch\nTravel,40.00,\"taxi, airport\"\nFood,7.25,coffee\nUtilities,60.00,power\n\n2. Create a second file expenses_changed.csv for the changed-data demonstration:\ncategory,amount,note\nBooks,30.00,\"text, used\"\nBooks,15.50,notebook\nFood,9.00,market\nTravel,20.00,bus\nUtilities,45.00,water\n\n3. Write the script so it:\n   a. Reads the CSV with a proper CSV reader (not by splitting on commas), so quoted fields that contain commas stay intact.\n   b. Treats a row as valid only if it has exactly the header's columns, the amount parses as a number, and the category is non-empty. Anything else is malformed.\n   c. Accumulates a running total per category and, at the end, prints each category with its total (for example, Food: 19.75).\n   d. For every malformed row, prints the row number and a concrete reason (for example, row 4: amount 'abc' is not a number), instead of skipping the row silently.\n\n4. Run the script on expenses.csv. Check by hand that each printed category total equals the sum of that category's valid amounts, and that no row was dropped without a printed reason.\n\n5. Run the same script, unchanged, on expenses_changed.csv. Check the totals again by hand. This is the changed-data demonstration: the same logic must still produce correct totals and correct malformed-row messages without edits.\n\n6. If you want to see the error path work, add one deliberately broken row (for example, amount 'abc' or a missing column) to a copy of the data and confirm the script names that row and reason. This step is optional.\n\nKeep it small: no database, no web interface, no external libraries beyond your language's standard CSV support.",
