              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 30,
                "likelyMinutes": 60,
                "highMinutes": 90
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "New changed circuits are solved with correct method choice, a valid selection reason, and a correct solution, with no assistance."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "This is the entire accepted outcome: an independent method-selection capability on changed circuits, tested against the contradiction in the self-report. It is diagnosed and, if the diagnostic confirms weakness, repaired with targeted work rather than a blanket re-course."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "diagnostic_set",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "diagnose"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Diagnostic set: five changed circuits with method choice and reason",
            "activityType": "Diagnostic problem set",
            "purpose": "Produce the missing evidence that the mastery claim is contradicted or supported, and locate whether errors come from choosing the wrong method or from executing a correctly chosen method.",
            "instructions": "Work alone, without notes or worked examples, and do not look up solution methods during the attempt. Solve these five changed circuits. For each one, first write the method you are choosing and one sentence on why that method's assumptions and circuit topology fit; then carry out the solution and write the final numeric answer with units. Use only methods you have actually been taught; if a circuit seems to demand something you were never taught, say so explicitly and stop on that item rather than inventing an advanced method. Circuits: (1) Two resistors R1 = 220 ohm and R2 = 330 ohm in series across a 9 V battery; find the current and the voltage across R2. (2) The same two resistors in parallel across the same 9 V battery; find the total current and the current through R1. (3) A 12 V source in series with a 100 ohm resistor and a 0.1 microfarad capacitor that has been connected long enough to reach steady state; find the capacitor voltage and the circuit current. (4) A 10 V source in series with a 1 kilohm resistor and a 10 millihenry inductor at steady state; find the inductor current and the voltage across the inductor. (5) A 5 V source with a 1 kilohm resistor in series with two parallel branches, each branch being a 2 kilohm resistor; find the voltage across the parallel section and the total current from the source. After finishing all five, check each answer against the actual circuit behaviour (for example by re-deriving the expected relationship) and mark each item correct or wrong; do not change your original method choice or reason. Bring the full record of choices, reasons, answers and checks to the comparison.",
